reference:
Montenegro uses the euro (€), even though it isn't in the EU or the eurozone. Bring euros or withdraw them there; you don't need to exchange anything else.

Cards in Kotor:
- Visa and Mastercard (often contactless and Apple/Google Pay) are widely accepted at hotels, most restaurants and cafés in the Old Town, larger shops, supermarkets and tour agencies. American Express is much less accepted.
- Card machines sometimes "don't work", especially at small guesthouses and family-run places, so don't rely on cards alone.

Carry some cash (about €50–100 in small notes) for:
- taxis (many are cash-only; agree the fare first or use an app like Red Taxi or Taxi Kotor);
- local and intercity buses (pay the driver or the station kiosk; the bus station also charges a small platform fee);
- small bakeries, kiosks, markets, beach loungers and boat trips;
- tips (10% is customary in restaurants, usually in cash);
- smaller apartments, which often want cash on arrival.

ATMs: use bank ATMs (CKB, Hipotekarna, NLB, Erste, Addiko) and avoid the yellow-blue Euronet machines, which charge high fees. Always choose to be charged in EUR rather than your home currency (decline "conversion") at ATMs and card terminals.
